despite the anticipation for breath of  the wild sequel  nintendo has been dead silent about the  upcoming games since the release of the  announcement trailer  with zelda's 35th anniversary coming up  we're getting the feeling that something  may be coming soon so we've taken the  time to reflect on the first breath of  the wild to find 9 things that we'd want  to see from the upcoming sequel  if you want to stay on top of the latest  breath of the wild news make sure to  like and subscribe because we'll be on  it as soon as anything drops  partner bullzelda can we not have zelda  as a damsel in distress anymore wouldn't  it be cool to just fight alongside  her in a more cooperative experience  while the nintendo online system isn't  the greatest  nintendo has generally been good about  having adventures together with friends  with games like luigi's mansion 3 so  zelda and link side by side out in the  open world sounds like a dream come true  other ideas like having an ai controlled  zelda fight alongside you  and outright playable zelda instead of  link or even controlling both characters  simultaneously a la astral chain would  be interesting additions  just make her more involved somehow more  traditional zelda items  bomb chews the gale boomerang hook shots  and so many traditional zelda equipment  pieces will be interesting additions to  the breath of the wild sequel  we appreciate the effort that went into  adding callback items to the original  breath of the wild such as the big goron  sword and the twilight bow but zelda  fans are hungry and we always want more  nero mechanics while outright devil may  cry combat mechanics in a mainline zelda  game would be a bit  too much this new arm possessed link  gives me some serious nero vibes  i'm hoping that the arm has some  grappling abilities for exploration  puzzling and combat because that would  be outright awesome  and in the same vein as some zelda  equipment that should return a spiritual  successor to the hook shot would be more  than welcome  more traditional zelda dungeons we  wouldn't want to replace shrines  far from it but we're hoping the main  dungeon contained more of what made the  original zelda so memorable  maps compasses keys unique bosses maybe  even specific dungeon items  while the divine beasts were great they  really just felt like giant shrines  and since the divine beasts are  presumably conquered now is a time for  dungeons  bigger emphasis on story despite  everything breath of the wild did well  its story really took a back seat  especially towards the end  a toe was dipped into this with age of  calamity and we feel like it could be  even better  because the game was so focused on  allowing you to tell your own adventure  and do anything in any order there was  little room for the characters and story  to grow  and since collecting all the memories  were an optional part of breath of the  wild  some players missed out on even more  context to the story now that we're onto  the sequel we're hoping the game decides  to put more of an emphasis on the story  and its characters  allowing them to fully develop  throughout the course of the game this  might mean making the story more linear  but if the rest of the game is still  open then we think this won't be too  much of a compromise  especially if the story is good nintendo  has proven in the past with titles such  as wind waker and skyward sword that  there are some great stories to be told  in the zelda universe and with breath of  the wild specifically there's so much  more to zelda link in their relationship  so the sequel seems like the best place  to do this  explore new areas outside of hyrule or a  different version  hyrule in breath of the wild is massive  and a great place to truly express a  sense of freedom the game gives you  but if you're exploring the same area in  the sequel how will that work for people  who have poured hundreds of hours into  the original  that sense of discovery will be gone  we're hoping that breath of the wild 2  has some sort of termina or coholint  island if it does take place in the same  hyrule  or at the very least has a dark world or  some sort of low rule variant  where things are mostly the same but  slightly different  swimming upgrades as much as the ground  and air movement was an astounding feat  in breath of the wild  i felt like swimming was somewhat of an  afterthought it was fairly clunky to  move quickly and there wasn't a dive  option  and we know the developers put so much  effort into making breath of the wild's  environment so beautiful  even unexplorable areas such as lowland  villages bay so  why weren't we able to explore it you  don't even have to let us fully dive  even the good old iron boost method  would be an interesting addition  return the dark nut there are plenty of  colorful other enemies and the zelda  series that should make a showing in the  breath of the wild sequel but our  personal vouch is for the return of dark  nuts  lynels were an amazing challenge in  breath of the wild but a good  old-fashioned tough one-on-one duel with  metal and metal action  is something that would bring a tear to  my eye in both twilight princess and  wind waker they're one of our favorite  enemies to fight  pet dogs enough said if we can't bed  dogs in the sequel  it's basically unplayable in all  seriousness though the dogs are pretty  darn cute and the feed for treasure  mechanic was cool but  just let me pet the good boys they  deserve it  so there you have it we're so excited  for the breath of the wild sequel that  we just can't stop thinking about it  what do you guys want to see in rest of  the world too  let us know in the comments below and  stay tuned for gamespot for any future  updates on breath of the wild 2.
 

